[FINAL-TEXT] **Root cause confirmed.** The full nftables ruleset on router6 shows a FORWARD chain with these rules for `iifname "eth2"` (the router3-facing link `r6_r3`):

1. `iifname "eth2" ip saddr 172.16.10.0/24 ip daddr 172.18.51.1 tcp dport 80 → accept` (narrow exception)
2. `iifname "eth2" meta l4proto tcp → drop` (all TCP)
3. `iifname "eth2" meta l4proto udp → drop` (all UDP)
4. `iifname "eth2" → drop` ← **catch-all: 24 packets / 2016 bytes already dropped** (our ICMP probes)

The chain's default policy is `accept`, so any traffic that *doesn't* come in on eth2 flows normally. But any return traffic arriving at router6 from router3 (eth2) — including ICMP echo replies from pc11/pc13 destined for pc15–pc18 — hits the catch-all drop. This is why pings from pc15–pc18 time out: the echo requests exit router6 toward router3 unimpeded (egress is not filtered), but the replies returning via eth2 are silently discarded.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The nftables `filter` table's `FORWARD` chain on router6, specifically three drop rules scoped to `iifname "eth2"` — the interface connecting router6 to router3 on link `r6_r3`.

**Mechanism:** Traffic from pc15–pc18 (172.18.51.x, reachable via mswitch2 → router6) to any destination on the router3 side (e.g., pc11/pc12 at 192.168.200.11/12, pc13/pc14 at 192.168.200.13/14) follows the forward path pc15 → switch10 → mswitch2 → router6(eth0) → eth2 → router3 → router4 → target. This direction is not filtered. The reply packets take the reverse path, re-entering router6 on eth2. Three FORWARD-chain rules then fire in sequence: TCP drops (l4proto tcp), UDP drops (l4proto udp), and a catch-all drop for everything else (ICMP, other). The catch-all alone has already discarded 24 packets (2016 bytes) — exactly the ICMP echo replies from the diagnostic pings. The only allowed exception is HTTP (TCP/80) from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1, which is harmlessly specific.

**Localization:** router6, FORWARD chain, three `iifname "eth2" … drop` rules. No other router or switch is implicated; pc4 and pc5 (routing through mswitch1 → router0 → router5/router1 path) are unaffected because their traffic never traverses router6.eth2.

**Root cause:** An intentionally or erroneously injected nftables ACL on router6 drops all forwarded traffic ingressing on the router3-facing interface (eth2), blocking all return traffic for hosts behind mswitch2/switch10.

**Fix:** On router6, delete the three drop rules from `table ip filter chain FORWARD`: `nft delete rule ip filter FORWARD` for the tcp-drop, udp-drop, and catch-all-drop entries (or flush the chain and restore only the intended accept rule). The default chain policy is `accept`, so removing the drops immediately restores full bidirectional forwarding for pc15–pc18.
=== END ===